# The farm's art: which tile of which sheet each semantic map id wears.
#
# TWO sheets, because they have two different origins and mixing them would lose
# that. `town` is Kenney's Tiny Town (CC0) imported from PNG; `water` is one tile
# this project drew in the Studio's Texture Lab. Both arrived as .hrt through their
# own command (asset.import / asset.texture) and neither knows the other exists —
# see assets/ATTRIBUTION.md. Compositing our pixel into Kenney's file would have made
# the result impossible to attribute and would be erased by the next re-import.
#
# town indices run left-to-right, top-to-bottom over a 12 x 11 grid of 16px tiles, so
# index = row * 12 + column. They were read off the sheet, not guessed: the first
# pass had the stones one tile to the left, which looked like dirt in the field.
#
# An id with no line here keeps the flat colour the game drew before there was art.
sheet town  textures/town.hrt       16
sheet water textures/farm_water.hrt 16
sheet path  textures/farm_path.hrt  16
sheet who   textures/farm_player.hrt 16
sheet anna  textures/farm_anna.hrt   16

tile ground 1 town  0      # plain grass
tile ground 3 water 0      # the pond — the tile the pack did not have

# The path is autotiled — but this file no longer says so. `rule 2 line` in
# maps/farm_home.map2 says it, because "a path is a road, not a region" is a fact
# about the world and the Map workspace has to be able to read it (chapter 134).
# Here, index 0 is just where the set starts: the sheet's grid position IS the
# neighbour mask, so tile 5 is north|south and the corner, the T and the two end caps
# are worked out per cell. Redraw the path in the Map workspace and the pieces follow.
tile ground 2 path 0       # the worn path, sixteen pieces we drew

tile decor 1 town 28       # a small green tree
tile decor 2 town 72       # timber wall
tile decor 3 town 43       # white stones in the grass

# The people. Neither of them was drawn: both are `textures/*.mix` — the same body and
# head from `parts_farm.pix`, and Anna is a hat plus two colour swaps on top. That is
# the fourth door into .hrt (chapter 135) and the reason it exists: a second villager
# costs three lines rather than a drawing, and looks like she is from the same village
# by construction rather than by care.
actor player who  0
actor anna   anna 0
